INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Dyspepsia & GORD','easy','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with a 3-month history of burning retrosternal pain worse when lying down at night and relieved by antacids. She has no dysphagia or weight loss. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD)","B. Peptic ulcer disease","C. Oesophageal carcinoma","D. Achalasia"]','A','The classic presentation of GORD includes burning retrosternal (heartburn) pain that worsens when lying down or bending forward and is relieved by antacids. The absence of alarm features (dysphagia, weight loss, anaemia) makes malignancy less likely. Peptic ulcer typically presents with epigastric pain. Achalasia causes progressive dysphagia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Dyspepsia & GORD','medium','mcq','A 58-year-old man presents with new-onset dyspepsia for 6 weeks, unintentional weight loss of 5 kg, and difficulty swallowing solids. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. Start a proton pump inhibitor empirically","B. Refer urgently for upper GI endoscopy","C. Perform H. pylori testing and treat if positive","D. Prescribe antacids and review in 4 weeks"]','B','This patient has alarm (red flag) symptoms: new-onset dyspepsia in a patient >55 years, progressive dysphagia, and significant unintentional weight loss. These features warrant urgent referral for upper GI endoscopy to exclude oesophageal or gastric malignancy. Empirical PPI therapy would be inappropriate without first excluding cancer.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Dyspepsia & GORD','easy','mcq','Which investigation is the gold standard for confirming GORD when the clinical diagnosis is uncertain?','["A. Barium swallow","B. Oesophageal manometry","C. 24-hour ambulatory oesophageal pH monitoring","D. Upper GI endoscopy"]','C','24-hour ambulatory oesophageal pH monitoring is the gold standard for confirming GORD, as it directly measures acid exposure time and correlates symptoms with reflux episodes. Endoscopy can show complications (oesophagitis, Barrett''''s) but is not positive in all GORD patients. Manometry assesses motility, not reflux.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Dyspepsia & GORD','medium','mcq','A 40-year-old woman with GORD has not responded to lifestyle measures and antacids. According to the Ghana STG, what is the recommended first-line pharmacological treatment?','["A. Ranitidine 150 mg twice daily","B. Sucralfate 1 g four times daily","C. Metoclopramide 10 mg three times daily","D. Omeprazole 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks"]','D','The Ghana STG recommends omeprazole 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks as first-line pharmacological therapy for GORD after lifestyle measures fail. PPIs are superior to H2-receptor antagonists for healing oesophagitis. Lifestyle advice includes elevating the head of the bed, avoiding fatty foods, caffeine, alcohol, smoking, and not eating within 3 hours of bedtime.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines 2017'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Dyspepsia & GORD','medium','mcq','Which of the following is NOT a recognised alarm symptom requiring urgent endoscopy in a patient with dyspepsia?','["A. Epigastric bloating after meals","B. Unintentional weight loss >5 kg","C. Progressive dysphagia","D. Unexplained iron-deficiency anaemia"]','A','Epigastric bloating after meals is a common dyspeptic symptom but is NOT an alarm feature. Alarm (red flag) symptoms mandating urgent endoscopy include: dysphagia, unintentional weight loss, iron-deficiency anaemia, persistent vomiting, palpable epigastric mass, and new-onset dyspepsia in patients >55 years.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Dyspepsia & GORD','hard','mcq','A 52-year-old man with a 15-year history of GORD managed with PPIs undergoes surveillance endoscopy that reveals a 4 cm segment of columnar-lined epithelium with intestinal metaplasia in the lower oesophagus. What is his most significant long-term risk?','["A. Oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma","B. Oesophageal adenocarcinoma","C. Gastric antral carcinoma","D. Oesophageal stricture"]','B','This patient has Barrett''''s oesophagus — a complication of chronic GORD characterised by replacement of normal squamous epithelium with columnar epithelium with intestinal metaplasia. The major risk is progression to oesophageal adenocarcinoma (not squamous cell carcinoma). Risk factors for progression include longer segment length, high-grade dysplasia, male sex, and obesity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Dyspepsia & GORD','medium','mcq','Which lifestyle change is MOST effective in reducing nocturnal oesophageal acid exposure in GORD?','["A. Reducing meal frequency to twice daily","B. Increasing dietary fibre intake","C. Elevating the head of the bed by 15-20 cm","D. Drinking more water with meals"]','C','Elevating the head of the bed (by 15-20 cm) is one of the most effective positional measures for nocturnal GORD, using gravity to reduce acid exposure. Other key lifestyle measures include weight loss (if overweight), avoiding fatty foods, caffeine, alcohol, smoking, eating smaller meals, and avoiding lying down within 3 hours of eating.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Dyspepsia & GORD','hard','mcq','A 48-year-old woman with GORD well-controlled on omeprazole 20 mg daily wants to try stopping her PPI. Which management strategy is appropriate?','["A. Abrupt cessation with no further treatment","B. Add H. pylori eradication therapy before stopping","C. Increase dose to omeprazole 40 mg before stopping","D. Switch to step-down therapy: reduce to on-demand or lowest effective dose"]','D','Step-down therapy is recommended for patients with well-controlled GORD: after 4-8 weeks of PPI therapy, step down to the lowest effective dose or switch to on-demand/intermittent dosing. Abrupt cessation can lead to rebound acid hypersecretion. H. pylori eradication is not indicated unless there is co-existing peptic ulcer disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Dyspepsia & GORD','easy','mcq','Which type of hiatus hernia is most commonly associated with GORD?','["A. Sliding hiatus hernia","B. Para-oesophageal (rolling) hiatus hernia","C. Incisional hernia","D. Mixed hiatus hernia"]','A','A sliding hiatus hernia (type I) is the most common type (>95% of hiatus hernias) and the type most commonly associated with GORD. The gastro-oesophageal junction moves above the diaphragm, impairing the lower oesophageal sphincter mechanism. Para-oesophageal hernias are less common and can cause obstruction and strangulation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Dyspepsia & GORD','hard','mcq','A 61-year-old woman with a 20-year history of GORD presents with 2 months of progressive dysphagia (solids then liquids) and 7 kg weight loss. Endoscopy shows an irregular, friable mass at the gastro-oesophageal junction. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma","B. Oesophageal adenocarcinoma arising in Barrett''s oesophagus","C. Benign oesophageal stricture","D. Achalasia"]','B','This patient has classic features of oesophageal adenocarcinoma: progressive dysphagia (solids to liquids), significant weight loss, long history of GORD, and an endoscopic mass at the GEJ. Oesophageal adenocarcinoma arises in Barrett''''s oesophagus and predominantly affects the lower oesophagus/GEJ. Squamous cell carcinoma occurs more in the mid-oesophagus and is associated with smoking and alcohol.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Peptic Ulcer Disease','easy','mcq','A 45-year-old man has epigastric pain worse when hungry and relieved by eating. No alarm symptoms. What is the most appropriate initial investigation to identify the most common underlying cause?','["A. Upper GI endoscopy with CLO test","B. Serum H. pylori IgG serology","C. Urea breath test for H. pylori","D. Faecal occult blood test"]','C','The urea breath test (UBT) is the best non-invasive test for active H. pylori infection. H. pylori is the most common cause of duodenal ulcer. Serology (IgG) remains positive even after eradication and cannot confirm active infection. The CLO (rapid urease) test requires endoscopic biopsy and is not the first-line test in a patient without alarm features.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Peptic Ulcer Disease','medium','mcq','A 50-year-old woman with a gastric ulcer tests positive for H. pylori and completes triple therapy. When is the most appropriate time to confirm eradication?','["A. 1 week after completing antibiotics","B. 3 months after completing antibiotics","C. Immediately after completing antibiotics using serology","D. 4-6 weeks after completing antibiotics, after stopping PPIs for 2 weeks"]','D','Test of cure should be performed 4-6 weeks after completing H. pylori eradication therapy. PPIs should be stopped at least 2 weeks before testing to avoid false-negative results. Serology (IgG antibodies) is NOT appropriate for test of cure as antibodies remain elevated for months to years after successful eradication. Urea breath test or stool antigen test are preferred.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Peptic Ulcer Disease','easy','mcq','A 60-year-old man on long-term NSAIDs presents with haematemesis. Endoscopy reveals a posterior duodenal ulcer. Which complication is characteristically associated with posterior duodenal ulcer?','["A. Haemorrhage from the gastroduodenal artery","B. Perforation with free gas under the diaphragm","C. Pyloric stenosis","D. Malignant transformation"]','A','A posterior duodenal ulcer characteristically erodes into the gastroduodenal artery, causing massive haemorrhage (haematemesis and/or melaena). An anterior duodenal ulcer typically perforates into the peritoneal cavity presenting with sudden severe pain and peritonitis. Pyloric stenosis can complicate either but haemorrhage is the classic complication of posterior wall involvement.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Peptic Ulcer Disease','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man on regular ibuprofen presents with sudden-onset severe epigastric pain, board-like abdominal rigidity, and absent bowel sounds. Which investigation would confirm the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Serum amylase","B. Erect chest X-ray","C. Abdominal ultrasound","D. CT abdomen with contrast"]','B','The presentation is classic for perforated peptic ulcer: sudden severe epigastric pain with board-like rigidity and peritonism. An erect chest X-ray showing free air (gas) under the diaphragm confirms pneumoperitoneum from perforation. This is a surgical emergency. Serum amylase is elevated in pancreatitis. CT abdomen is more sensitive but erect CXR is the first-line investigation in this setting.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Peptic Ulcer Disease','hard','mcq','Which H. pylori testing method is NOT appropriate for confirming successful eradication after treatment?','["A. 13C-urea breath test","B. Stool H. pylori antigen test","C. Serum H. pylori IgG serology","D. CLO (rapid urease) test on repeat endoscopic biopsy"]','C','Serum IgG serology for H. pylori remains positive for months to years after successful eradication and therefore CANNOT be used to confirm eradication. Appropriate tests of cure include: 13C-urea breath test, stool antigen test, and CLO test on repeat biopsy (all require active infection for a positive result). PPIs should be stopped 2 weeks before the UBT or stool antigen test to avoid false-negative results.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Peptic Ulcer Disease','medium','mcq','A 42-year-old woman has a 1.5 cm gastric ulcer on endoscopy with no malignancy on biopsy and confirmed H. pylori infection. What is the most appropriate management?','["A. Omeprazole alone for 8 weeks","B. H2-receptor antagonist alone for 6 weeks","C. Immediate surgical referral for gastrectomy","D. H. pylori eradication therapy followed by PPI for 4-8 weeks, then repeat endoscopy"]','D','A gastric ulcer always requires repeat endoscopy after treatment to confirm healing and exclude malignancy. Management: eradicate H. pylori with triple therapy, continue PPI for 4-8 weeks, then repeat endoscopy at 6-8 weeks to confirm ulcer healing and review biopsies. Non-healing or suspicious ulcers require further biopsies or surgery.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Peptic Ulcer Disease','hard','mcq','A 68-year-old man on aspirin and ibuprofen presents with melaena. H. pylori testing is negative. After ulcer healing, what is the best strategy to prevent recurrence while maintaining cardiovascular protection?','["A. Continue aspirin, switch ibuprofen to a COX-2 inhibitor, and add PPI","B. Stop aspirin and continue ibuprofen with PPI cover","C. Stop all NSAIDs and antiplatelet therapy","D. Switch aspirin to clopidogrel and continue ibuprofen"]','A','In a patient requiring ongoing antiplatelet therapy (aspirin for cardiovascular protection): (1) continue low-dose aspirin as it is essential; (2) switch the traditional NSAID to a COX-2 selective inhibitor (lower GI risk); (3) add a PPI for gastroprotection. Stopping aspirin in a high-cardiovascular-risk patient could be harmful. H. pylori-negative NSAID ulcer prevention relies on co-prescription of PPI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Peptic Ulcer Disease','medium','mcq','A 38-year-old man with a duodenal ulcer caused by H. pylori is prescribed triple therapy (omeprazole, amoxicillin, clarithromycin) for 7 days. What is the expected eradication rate with this regimen?','["A. 50-60%","B. 80-90%","C. 65-75%","D. >95%"]','B','Standard triple therapy (PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin or metronidazole for 7-14 days) achieves H. pylori eradication rates of 80-90% in areas with low clarithromycin resistance. Eradication rates have declined in some regions due to rising antibiotic resistance. In areas of high clarithromycin resistance, quadruple (bismuth-based) therapy is preferred.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Peptic Ulcer Disease','easy','mcq','A patient with peptic ulcer disease presents with vomiting large amounts of partially digested food and visible gastric peristalsis. What complication has most likely developed?','["A. Peptic ulcer perforation","B. Bleeding duodenal ulcer","C. Gastric outlet obstruction (pyloric stenosis)","D. Penetrating ulcer into the pancreas"]','C','Vomiting of large volumes of partially digested or undigested food, visible gastric peristalsis (succussion splash may be present), and a history of peptic ulcer disease are classic features of gastric outlet obstruction (pyloric stenosis). This complication results from scarring at the pylorus from recurrent ulceration and typically causes non-bilious vomiting and metabolic alkalosis.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Peptic Ulcer Disease','hard','mcq','A 72-year-old man with a bleeding duodenal ulcer has Hb 7.2 g/dL. After resuscitation, endoscopy shows active spurting from the ulcer base. What is the most appropriate endoscopic treatment?','["A. Injection of adrenaline alone","B. Thermal coagulation alone","C. Endoscopic band ligation","D. Dual endoscopic therapy (adrenaline injection plus thermal or mechanical therapy)"]','D','Current evidence and guidelines recommend dual endoscopic therapy for high-risk stigmata of haemorrhage (active spurting, visible non-bleeding vessel): adrenaline injection alone is inferior to combination therapy. Dual therapy combines adrenaline injection with thermal coagulation (heater probe, argon plasma coagulation) or mechanical haemostasis (clips). This achieves haemostasis and reduces re-bleeding rates. Band ligation is used for variceal bleeding.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute GI Bleeding','easy','mcq','A 65-year-old man presents with haematemesis and melaena. BP 85/50 mmHg, pulse 115 bpm. What is the most immediate priority?','["A. Two large-bore IV cannulae, IV fluid resuscitation and cross-match blood","B. IV omeprazole infusion","C. Urgent upper GI endoscopy within 1 hour","D. Nasogastric tube insertion to confirm bleeding site"]','A','The immediate priority in haemodynamically unstable acute GI bleeding is resuscitation: insert two large-bore IV cannulae (16G or larger), commence IV fluid resuscitation (crystalloid while awaiting blood), and send blood for FBC, U&E, LFTs, coagulation, and cross-match. Endoscopy is important but should be performed after initial resuscitation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute GI Bleeding','medium','mcq','A cirrhotic patient presents with haematemesis. Which combination of treatments should be started BEFORE endoscopy in suspected variceal haemorrhage?','["A. IV omeprazole and fresh frozen plasma","B. Terlipressin and prophylactic IV antibiotics","C. Octreotide and IV vitamin K","D. Propranolol and lactulose"]','B','In suspected variceal haemorrhage, evidence-based pre-endoscopy treatment includes: (1) Terlipressin — a vasopressin analogue that reduces splanchnic blood flow and portal pressure; and (2) Prophylactic broad-spectrum antibiotics (ciprofloxacin or ceftriaxone) — which reduce infection risk and improve survival. These should be started as soon as variceal bleeding is suspected, before endoscopy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute GI Bleeding','medium','mcq','The pre-endoscopy Rockall score for acute upper GI bleeding uses which three variables?','["A. Age, haemoglobin, blood pressure","B. Age, endoscopic diagnosis, evidence of bleeding","C. Age, shock status, comorbidity","D. Pulse rate, platelet count, urea level"]','C','The pre-endoscopy Rockall score uses: (1) Age (<60=0, 60-79=1, ≥80=2); (2) Shock (no shock=0, tachycardia=1, hypotension=2); (3) Comorbidity (none=0, cardiac failure/IHD=2, renal/liver failure/metastatic cancer=3). The full post-endoscopy Rockall score adds endoscopic diagnosis and stigmata of haemorrhage. A score of 0 indicates very low re-bleeding/mortality risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute GI Bleeding','easy','mcq','A 70-year-old woman presents with fresh rectal bleeding (haematochezia) and no haematemesis. Her haemodynamics are stable. What is the most likely source of bleeding?','["A. Peptic ulcer disease","B. Oesophageal varices","C. Mallory-Weiss tear","D. Lower gastrointestinal source"]','D','Fresh rectal bleeding (haematochezia) without haematemesis in a haemodynamically stable patient indicates a lower GI source. Common causes include diverticular disease, haemorrhoids, angiodysplasia, colorectal cancer, and IBD. Upper GI bleeding can present with haematochezia only if very brisk. Haematemesis, coffee-ground vomiting, and melaena indicate upper GI sources.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute GI Bleeding','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man with haematemesis has a post-endoscopy Rockall score of 7. Endoscopy shows a duodenal ulcer with a visible non-bleeding vessel. He is haemodynamically stable after resuscitation. What is the most appropriate next step after endoscopic haemostasis?','["A. Admit to HDU/ICU, commence IV PPI infusion (omeprazole 80 mg bolus then 8 mg/hr for 72 hours), and reassess","B. Discharge home with oral PPI and outpatient review","C. Proceed immediately to surgery","D. Arrange CT angiography within 24 hours"]','A','A Rockall score ≥5 indicates high risk of re-bleeding and mortality. After endoscopic haemostasis of a high-risk lesion (visible vessel), the patient should be admitted to a high-dependency setting, commenced on high-dose IV PPI infusion (omeprazole 80 mg bolus then 8 mg/hr for 72 hours), kept nil by mouth, and monitored closely. Surgery or interventional radiology is reserved for failed endoscopic haemostasis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute GI Bleeding','medium','mcq','At what haemoglobin threshold should blood transfusion be considered in a haemodynamically stable patient with acute non-variceal upper GI bleeding?','["A. Hb <10 g/dL","B. Hb <7 g/dL","C. Hb <8 g/dL","D. Hb <9 g/dL"]','B','Current NICE and BSG guidelines recommend a restrictive transfusion strategy: transfuse when Hb <7 g/dL, targeting Hb 7-9 g/dL. A higher threshold of Hb <8 g/dL is used in patients with acute coronary syndrome or significant cardiovascular disease. Restrictive transfusion reduces re-bleeding rates compared to liberal transfusion strategies, particularly in variceal bleeding (over-transfusion increases portal pressure).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute GI Bleeding','easy','mcq','A 35-year-old man has haematemesis following severe vomiting after heavy alcohol use. Endoscopy reveals a linear mucosal tear at the gastro-oesophageal junction. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Boerhaave syndrome","B. Dieulafoy lesion","C. Mallory-Weiss tear","D. Cameron lesion"]','C','A Mallory-Weiss tear is a linear mucosal laceration at the gastro-oesophageal junction caused by sudden increase in intragastric pressure (retching, vomiting, coughing). It classically presents with haematemesis following forceful vomiting, often after alcohol excess. Bleeding is usually self-limiting but can be severe. Boerhaave syndrome is a full-thickness oesophageal rupture (surgical emergency).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute GI Bleeding','hard','mcq','A 50-year-old cirrhotic patient has acute variceal haemorrhage controlled with band ligation, but bleeding recurs. What is the next most appropriate intervention?','["A. Repeat endoscopy with sclerotherapy","B. Surgical portocaval shunt","C. Vasopressin infusion","D. Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS)"]','D','TIPS (Transjugular Intrahepatic Portosystemic Shunt) is the recommended salvage therapy for variceal haemorrhage that fails endoscopic band ligation plus vasoactive drugs. TIPS reduces portal pressure by creating a shunt between the portal and hepatic veins, controlling bleeding in >90% of cases. It is also considered early (within 72h) in high-risk patients (Child-Pugh C or B with active bleeding).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute GI Bleeding','medium','mcq','A 78-year-old woman on warfarin (INR 4.5) for atrial fibrillation presents with melaena. After resuscitation, what is the most appropriate approach to her anticoagulation?','["A. Stop warfarin, give IV vitamin K, and consider prothrombin complex concentrate if haemodynamically unstable","B. Continue warfarin and transfuse packed red cells","C. Reverse warfarin with fresh frozen plasma alone","D. Switch to low-molecular-weight heparin immediately"]','A','In anticoagulated patients with acute GI bleeding: (1) Stop warfarin immediately; (2) Give IV vitamin K (5-10 mg) for reversal; (3) If haemodynamically unstable or requiring urgent endoscopy/surgery, give prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC — faster and more complete reversal than FFP). FFP is second-line if PCC is unavailable.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute GI Bleeding','medium','mcq','What is the recommended timing for endoscopy in a haemodynamically stabilised patient with acute upper GI bleeding?','["A. Within 1 hour of presentation","B. Within 24 hours of presentation","C. Within 6 hours of presentation","D. Within 72 hours of presentation"]','B','Current NICE and BSG guidelines recommend endoscopy within 24 hours of presentation for haemodynamically stable patients with acute upper GI bleeding, after appropriate resuscitation. Immediate endoscopy (within 1-2 hours) is recommended for haemodynamically unstable patients or clinical concern about variceal bleeding. Out-of-hours endoscopy within 24 hours does not worsen outcomes in stable patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','IBD - Clinical Management','easy','mcq','A 25-year-old woman has a 3-month history of bloody diarrhoea 6-8 times/day, urgency, and tenesmus. Sigmoidoscopy shows continuous mucosal inflammation from the rectum extending proximally. Biopsy shows crypt abscesses. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Crohn''s disease","B. Infectious colitis","C. Ulcerative colitis","D. Colorectal carcinoma"]','C','Ulcerative colitis (UC) characteristically presents with bloody diarrhoea, urgency, and tenesmus. Key features: continuous mucosal inflammation starting in the rectum and extending proximally, crypt abscesses on biopsy, and superficial mucosal involvement only (unlike Crohn''''s, which is transmural). Crohn''''s shows skip lesions, can affect any part of the GI tract, and has granulomas on biopsy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','IBD - Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old man with Crohn''''s disease has a flare with CRP 85 mg/L and faecal calprotectin 850 µg/g. What investigation is most useful for assessing the extent of small bowel Crohn''''s disease?','["A. Colonoscopy with ileoscopy","B. Barium follow-through","C. CT abdomen with contrast","D. MRI enterography"]','D','MRI enterography is the preferred investigation for assessing small bowel Crohn''''s disease: it provides excellent soft tissue detail, can assess mural thickness and disease activity, and identifies complications (strictures, fistulae, abscesses) without radiation exposure. Small bowel follow-through (barium) is less sensitive and uses radiation. CT is useful in acute complications. Colonoscopy with ileoscopy assesses the terminal ileum and colon.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','IBD - Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old woman with extensive UC has a moderate flare (4-6 bloody stools/day, CRP 45, Hb 10.5 g/dL). What is the most appropriate induction therapy?','["A. Oral prednisolone 40 mg/day","B. Mesalazine (5-ASA) 2.4 g/day orally","C. Azathioprine 2.5 mg/kg/day","D. Infliximab 5 mg/kg IV"]','A','For moderate-to-severe UC flares, oral corticosteroids (prednisolone 40 mg/day, tapering over 8 weeks) are the standard induction therapy. 5-ASA is used for mild disease and maintenance. Azathioprine is a maintenance agent (slow onset, not suitable for acute induction). Infliximab (anti-TNF) is reserved for steroid-refractory or steroid-dependent disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','IBD - Clinical Management','hard','mcq','Which serological marker is characteristically associated with ulcerative colitis rather than Crohn''''s disease?','["A. Anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibody (ASCA)","B. Perinuclear anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (pANCA)","C. Anti-CBir1 flagellin antibody","D. Anti-OmpC antibody"]','B','pANCA (perinuclear ANCA) is associated with ulcerative colitis (present in ~60-70% of UC patients). ASCA (anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibody) is associated with Crohn''''s disease. The ASCA+/pANCA- pattern suggests Crohn''''s, while ASCA-/pANCA+ suggests UC. These serological tests have limited sensitivity but can help differentiate IBD subtypes in indeterminate cases.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','IBD - Clinical Management','easy','mcq','What is the gold standard investigation for diagnosing inflammatory bowel disease?','["A. Abdominal CT scan","B. Stool culture and sensitivity","C. Colonoscopy with biopsy","D. Faecal calprotectin level"]','C','Colonoscopy with biopsy is the gold standard for diagnosing and classifying IBD. It allows direct visualisation of mucosal changes, assessment of disease extent, and tissue sampling for histological diagnosis. Faecal calprotectin is a sensitive marker of intestinal inflammation useful for screening and monitoring, but is not diagnostic. CT and MRI assess complications and disease extent but cannot replace histology.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','IBD - Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old man with UC on mesalazine has had two relapses requiring steroids in the past year. What is the most appropriate maintenance therapy to add?','["A. Continue mesalazine alone and increase the dose","B. Proceed to colectomy","C. Start long-term prednisolone at low dose","D. Add azathioprine or 6-mercaptopurine"]','D','In UC patients who are steroid-dependent or have frequent relapses (≥2 per year), immunomodulator therapy with azathioprine (2.5 mg/kg/day) or 6-mercaptopurine should be added for maintenance. Long-term steroids are not appropriate due to side effects. Anti-TNF biologics are used if immunomodulators fail. Colectomy is considered for refractory disease or dysplasia/cancer.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','IBD - Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A 40-year-old woman with extensive UC presents with bloody diarrhoea >10 times/day, HR 110, temperature 38.5°C, CRP 140 mg/L. Abdominal X-ray shows colonic dilatation with transverse colon diameter of 7 cm. What is the immediate management priority?','["A. Toxic megacolon; urgent surgical review and IV corticosteroids","B. Moderately active UC; start oral prednisolone","C. Infective colitis; commence antibiotics and stool culture","D. Crohn''s colitis; commence infliximab infusion"]','A','Toxic megacolon is defined as acute non-obstructive colonic dilatation (diameter ≥6 cm on X-ray) with systemic toxicity (fever, tachycardia, leucocytosis). This is a surgical emergency with high mortality if perforation occurs. Immediate management: nil by mouth, IV corticosteroids (hydrocortisone 100 mg QDS), IV fluids, thromboprophylaxis, bowel rest, and urgent surgical review. If no improvement in 24-72 hours, emergency colectomy is indicated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','IBD - Clinical Management','medium','mcq','Which statement about surgical management of UC versus Crohn''''s disease is correct?','["A. Colectomy is curative in Crohn''s disease but not in UC","B. Surgery is curative in UC (panproctocolectomy) but Crohn''s disease frequently recurs after resection","C. Both UC and Crohn''s disease are cured by colectomy","D. Surgery is never indicated in UC; medical therapy is always preferred"]','B','Panproctocolectomy is curative in UC since the disease is limited to the colorectum. In contrast, Crohn''''s disease can affect any part of the GI tract; surgery is NOT curative and disease frequently recurs, often at the anastomosis site. Surgery in Crohn''''s is reserved for complications: strictures, fistulae, abscess, refractory disease, or perforation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','IBD - Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A patient with Crohn''''s disease has failed two courses of oral prednisolone and has been on azathioprine for 6 months with inadequate response. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. Add methotrexate to azathioprine","B. Increase azathioprine dose and add mesalazine","C. Switch to infliximab or adalimumab (anti-TNF therapy)","D. Refer for surgical resection"]','C','In Crohn''''s disease that is steroid-refractory or steroid-dependent despite adequate immunomodulator therapy, anti-TNF biological therapy (infliximab 5 mg/kg IV or adalimumab SC) is indicated. Anti-TNF agents achieve remission in ~50% of steroid-refractory patients. Ustekinumab and vedolizumab are alternatives. Methotrexate can be used as second-line immunomodulator if azathioprine is not tolerated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','IBD - Clinical Management','easy','mcq','A patient with IBD on long-term azathioprine has a WBC of 2.1 × 10⁹/L. What is the most appropriate immediate action?','["A. Continue azathioprine and repeat FBC in 4 weeks","B. Reduce azathioprine dose by 50%","C. Add filgrastim (G-CSF) and continue azathioprine","D. Stop azathioprine immediately and seek urgent haematology review"]','D','Azathioprine-induced leucopenia (WBC <3.0 × 10⁹/L) is a serious side effect that increases infection risk. Azathioprine should be stopped immediately if significant leucopenia occurs. FBC should be monitored regularly during therapy. TPMT enzyme activity should be checked before starting azathioprine to identify patients at high risk of myelosuppression.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Diarrhoea & Gastroenteritis','easy','mcq','A 3-year-old child in Ghana presents with profuse watery diarrhoea (10 episodes/day) and vomiting for 2 days. He is lethargic with sunken eyes and decreased skin turgor. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?','["A. Oral rehydration solution (ORS) given slowly in small amounts","B. Intravenous ceftriaxone 50 mg/kg/day","C. Loperamide 2 mg after each loose stool","D. Intravenous fluids with 5% dextrose and potassium chloride"]','A','Acute gastroenteritis with dehydration in a child should be managed with WHO low-osmolarity ORS as first-line therapy (sodium 75, glucose 75, chloride 65, potassium 20, citrate 10 mmol/L) according to the Ghana STG. ORS should be given in small, frequent amounts. Antibiotics are not indicated for most viral gastroenteritis. Loperamide is contraindicated in children. IV fluids are reserved for severe dehydration or shock.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines 2017'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Diarrhoea & Gastroenteritis','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old man returns from West Africa with 5 days of watery diarrhoea and abdominal cramps. Stool culture grows enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (ETEC). What is the most appropriate management?','["A. Ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for 5 days","B. Oral rehydration and supportive care; antibiotics only if severe or immunocompromised","C. Metronidazole 400 mg three times daily for 5 days","D. Ceftriaxone 1 g IV for 3 days"]','B','ETEC (enterotoxigenic E. coli) is the commonest cause of traveller''''s diarrhoea. It typically causes watery, self-limiting diarrhoea lasting 3-5 days. Management is primarily supportive with ORS. Antibiotics are reserved for severe disease, prolonged symptoms, or immunocompromised patients. Empirical antibiotic use promotes resistance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Diarrhoea & Gastroenteritis','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old woman in Ghana presents with sudden-onset profuse rice-water stools and severe dehydration from a flooded area. Which organism is the most likely cause, and what is the antibiotic of choice for adults?','["A. Shigella dysenteriae; ciprofloxacin","B. Salmonella typhi; ceftriaxone","C. Vibrio cholerae; doxycycline","D. Clostridium difficile; metronidazole"]','C','Rice-water stools (profuse, colourless watery stools), severe dehydration, and an epidemic setting (flooding) are characteristic of cholera caused by Vibrio cholerae. According to the Ghana STG, adults should receive doxycycline (single dose) or tetracycline as antibiotic treatment, in addition to aggressive fluid resuscitation. Children and pregnant women should receive azithromycin.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines 2017'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Diarrhoea & Gastroenteritis','easy','mcq','A 5-year-old child presents with fever, bloody diarrhoea, and abdominal cramps. Stool microscopy shows pus cells. Which organism is the most likely cause of this dysenteric illness in sub-Saharan Africa?','["A. Rotavirus","B. Salmonella typhi","C. Vibrio cholerae","D. Shigella dysenteriae"]','D','Bloody diarrhoea (dysentery) with fever, tenesmus, and pus cells in stool is characteristic of shigellosis (bacillary dysentery). Shigella is the most common cause of bacterial dysentery in children in sub-Saharan Africa. Treatment: ciprofloxacin (adults) or azithromycin (children). Rotavirus causes watery (not bloody) diarrhoea. Vibrio cholerae causes rice-water stools.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines 2017'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Diarrhoea & Gastroenteritis','hard','mcq','A hospitalised patient develops profuse watery diarrhoea after clindamycin therapy. Stool testing confirms Clostridioides difficile infection. What is the first-line treatment for mild-moderate CDI?','["A. Oral vancomycin 125 mg four times daily for 10-14 days","B. Metronidazole 400 mg three times daily for 10-14 days","C. IV vancomycin 500 mg four times daily for 10 days","D. Fidaxomicin 200 mg twice daily for 10 days"]','A','Current IDSA/SHEA (2017) guidelines recommend oral vancomycin 125 mg QDS for 10 days as first-line treatment for mild-moderate Clostridioides difficile infection, having replaced metronidazole due to superior clinical cure rates and lower recurrence. Fidaxomicin is an alternative with lower recurrence rates. IV vancomycin has poor luminal penetration and is not effective for CDI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Diarrhoea & Gastroenteritis','medium','mcq','A 2-year-old child in Ghana has diarrhoea with mild thirst and moist mucous membranes. According to the Ghana STG dehydration classification, what does this represent?','["A. No dehydration","B. Some (mild-moderate) dehydration","C. Severe dehydration","D. Hypernatraemic dehydration"]','B','The WHO/Ghana STG dehydration assessment: (1) No dehydration: well, drinks normally, normal eyes/skin turgor; (2) Some dehydration: restless or irritable, thirsty/drinks eagerly, sunken eyes, reduced skin turgor; (3) Severe dehydration: lethargic/unconscious, not able to drink, very sunken eyes, very slow skin pinch. This child has mild thirst but is alert with moist membranes — consistent with some (mild-moderate) dehydration. Treat with ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 hours.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines 2017'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Diarrhoea & Gastroenteritis','medium','mcq','Which scenario represents an APPROPRIATE indication for antibiotic therapy in acute diarrhoeal illness?','["A. 3-year-old child with rotavirus gastroenteritis and watery diarrhoea for 2 days","B. 25-year-old traveller with mild watery diarrhoea for 2 days after eating street food","C. 35-year-old immunocompromised patient with severe Salmonella enteritis and bacteraemia","D. 40-year-old man with non-typhi Salmonella enteritis and 3 days of self-limiting symptoms"]','C','Antibiotics in acute diarrhoea are indicated for: Shigella dysentery; cholera; typhoid fever; C. difficile; severe systemic illness/bacteraemia; immunocompromised patients. Antibiotics are NOT recommended for: rotavirus (viral), mild ETEC traveller''''s diarrhoea, or uncomplicated non-typhi Salmonella (prolongs the carrier state without clinical benefit in healthy individuals).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Diarrhoea & Gastroenteritis','easy','mcq','A 35-year-old man presents with 10 days of step-wise fever, rose-coloured spots on his abdomen, and relative bradycardia (pulse 65 despite temperature 39°C). What is the most likely diagnosis and first-line treatment?','["A. Malaria; artemether-lumefantrine","B. Meningococcal disease; benzylpenicillin","C. Amoebic dysentery; metronidazole","D. Typhoid fever (Salmonella typhi); ciprofloxacin or ceftriaxone"]','D','Typhoid fever (enteric fever) caused by Salmonella typhi presents with step-wise fever, relative bradycardia (Faget sign), rose spots (salmon-coloured macules on the trunk), splenomegaly, and systemic toxicity. Blood culture is the gold standard. Treatment: ciprofloxacin (where sensitive) or ceftriaxone IV for severe disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Diarrhoea & Gastroenteritis','hard','mcq','A 6-month-old infant presents with bloody diarrhoea and abdominal pain. On examination, a sausage-shaped mass is palpable in the right upper quadrant. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Intussusception","B. Shigella dysentery","C. Malrotation with volvulus","D. Hirschsprung''s disease"]','A','Intussusception (telescoping of one bowel segment into another) classically presents in infants 6-18 months with episodic colicky abdominal pain, drawing up of legs, ''''redcurrant jelly'''' stools (bloody mucous stools), and a palpable sausage-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant. Treatment: air or hydrostatic enema reduction (first-line); surgery if enema fails or peritonitis is present.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Diarrhoea & Gastroenteritis','medium','mcq','Which components are included in the WHO low-osmolarity oral rehydration solution (ORS) recommended by the Ghana STG?','["A. Sodium 90 mmol/L, glucose 111 mmol/L, total osmolarity 311 mmol/L","B. Sodium 75 mmol/L, glucose 75 mmol/L, total osmolarity 245 mmol/L","C. Sodium 60 mmol/L, glucose 90 mmol/L, total osmolarity 200 mmol/L","D. Sodium 50 mmol/L, glucose 100 mmol/L, total osmolarity 220 mmol/L"]','B','The WHO/UNICEF low-osmolarity ORS (recommended by Ghana STG) has: sodium 75 mmol/L, glucose 75 mmol/L, chloride 65 mmol/L, potassium 20 mmol/L, trisodium citrate 10 mmol/L, total osmolarity 245 mmol/L. This replaced the original high-osmolarity ORS (sodium 90, osmolarity 311) as it reduces stool output and vomiting and shortens diarrhoea duration.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines 2017'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Chronic Diarrhoea & Malabsorption','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old woman has a 6-month history of bloating, intermittent abdominal pain relieved by defaecation, and alternating constipation and diarrhoea. FBC, CRP, and coeliac screen are normal. Colonoscopy is normal. What is the most appropriate diagnosis?','["A. Crohn''s disease","B. Microscopic colitis","C. Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)","D. Coeliac disease"]','C','This patient meets the Rome IV criteria for IBS: recurrent abdominal pain ≥1 day/week for ≥3 months, associated with defaecation, change in stool frequency, or change in stool form, with normal investigations excluding organic pathology. IBS is a functional disorder. Management: reassurance, antispasmodics (mebeverine), low-FODMAP diet, and tricyclics/SSRIs for refractory symptoms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Chronic Diarrhoea & Malabsorption','easy','mcq','A 28-year-old woman with chronic diarrhoea, weight loss, and iron-deficiency anaemia has markedly elevated anti-tissue transglutaminase (TTG) IgA antibodies. What is the next best confirmatory investigation?','["A. Hydrogen breath test","B. Faecal elastase-1","C. CT enterography","D. Small bowel biopsy via upper GI endoscopy"]','D','Coeliac disease is strongly suggested by elevated anti-TTG IgA antibodies. Small bowel biopsy (duodenal biopsy at upper GI endoscopy) showing villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia (Marsh classification Grade 3) is the confirmatory investigation and gold standard. Treatment: strict gluten-free diet for life.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Chronic Diarrhoea & Malabsorption','hard','mcq','A 50-year-old man with heavy alcohol use presents with recurrent upper abdominal pain, steatorrhoea, and weight loss. Abdominal X-ray shows pancreatic calcification. What investigation best confirms exocrine pancreatic insufficiency?','["A. Faecal elastase-1","B. Serum lipase","C. Serum amylase","D. 72-hour faecal fat collection"]','A','Faecal elastase-1 (FE-1) is the preferred non-invasive test for exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (EPI). FE-1 <200 µg/g indicates moderate-to-severe EPI. It is not affected by pancreatic enzyme supplementation. Serum amylase and lipase are typically normal in chronic (as opposed to acute) pancreatitis. 72-hour faecal fat is sensitive but cumbersome.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Chronic Diarrhoea & Malabsorption','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man from West Africa has chronic diarrhoea, bloating, weight loss, and megaloblastic anaemia with low serum folate and vitamin B12. Duodenal biopsy shows partial villous atrophy but anti-TTG IgA is negative. He responds to folic acid and tetracycline. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Coeliac disease","B. Tropical sprue","C. Giardiasis","D. Whipple''s disease"]','B','Tropical sprue is a malabsorption syndrome occurring in tropical regions. It presents with chronic diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, and nutritional deficiencies (particularly folate and B12). Small bowel biopsy shows partial villous atrophy (unlike coeliac, TTG is negative). It characteristically responds to folic acid supplementation combined with tetracycline (3-6 months). Coeliac would be TTG-positive.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Chronic Diarrhoea & Malabsorption','easy','mcq','A 35-year-old woman has watery diarrhoea, bloating, and flatulence after drinking from a stream on a camping trip. Stool microscopy shows oval cysts with 4 nuclei. What is the treatment?','["A. Ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for 5 days","B. Azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 3 days","C. Metronidazole 400 mg three times daily for 5-7 days","D. Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days"]','C','Giardia lamblia infection presents with watery, foul-smelling diarrhoea, bloating, and flatulence, often after drinking contaminated water. Stool microscopy shows oval cysts with 4 nuclei (diagnostic) or trophozoites. Treatment: metronidazole 400 mg TDS for 5-7 days (or tinidazole as single 2 g dose).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Chronic Diarrhoea & Malabsorption','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man presents with diarrhoea, arthralgia, weight loss over 2 years, and cognitive decline. Small bowel biopsy shows PAS-positive foamy macrophages in the lamina propria. What is the most likely diagnosis and causative organism?','["A. Coeliac disease; gluten sensitivity","B. Crohn''s disease; unknown aetiology","C. Mycobacterium avium complex; M. avium","D. Whipple''s disease; Tropheryma whipplei"]','D','Whipple''''s disease is a rare systemic infection caused by Tropheryma whipplei. Classic tetrad: diarrhoea/malabsorption, weight loss, arthralgia (often preceding GI symptoms by years), and fever. CNS involvement (cognitive decline, ophthalmoplegia) can occur. Pathognomonic finding: PAS-positive foamy macrophages in the small bowel lamina propria on biopsy. Treatment: ceftriaxone 2 g IV for 2 weeks followed by co-trimoxazole for 1 year.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Chronic Diarrhoea & Malabsorption','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old man with previous gastric surgery presents with diarrhoea, bloating, and vitamin B12 deficiency. A hydrogen breath test shows an early hydrogen peak after lactulose ingestion. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO)","B. Lactase deficiency","C. Coeliac disease","D. Chronic pancreatitis"]','A','Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO) is characterised by excessive bacteria in the small bowel, causing fermentation of carbohydrates and producing hydrogen gas. Risk factors include gastric surgery, blind loops, strictures, and achlorhydria. The hydrogen breath test shows an early (small bowel) peak of hydrogen after lactulose. Vitamin B12 deficiency occurs as bacteria consume B12. Treatment: rifaximin (or rotating antibiotics).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Chronic Diarrhoea & Malabsorption','hard','mcq','A 40-year-old woman with IBS-D has failed antispasmodics. Which dietary intervention has the strongest evidence for symptom reduction in IBS?','["A. Gluten-free diet","B. Low-FODMAP diet","C. High-fibre diet","D. Low-fat diet"]','B','The low-FODMAP diet (Fermentable Oligosaccharides, Disaccharides, Monosaccharides And Polyols) has the strongest evidence base for symptom relief in IBS, particularly bloating, diarrhoea, and abdominal pain. It involves restricting fermentable carbohydrates poorly absorbed and fermented by gut bacteria. It should be implemented under dietitian supervision with a structured reintroduction phase.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Chronic Diarrhoea & Malabsorption','medium','mcq','A 22-year-old woman with confirmed coeliac disease is placed on a gluten-free diet. After 3 months, which test best confirms mucosal healing?','["A. Anti-TTG IgA level (should normalise)","B. Faecal calprotectin level","C. Repeat duodenal biopsy showing recovery of villi","D. Serum albumin normalisation"]','C','Mucosal healing in coeliac disease is confirmed by repeat duodenal biopsy showing recovery of villous architecture (reduction in Marsh grade). Anti-TTG IgA levels decline with adherence to a gluten-free diet and are useful for monitoring compliance, but normalisation can take 12-24 months. Anti-TTG is not a substitute for histological confirmation of mucosal recovery.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Chronic Diarrhoea & Malabsorption','easy','mcq','A 45-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis from heavy alcohol use has steatorrhoea and weight loss. Which treatment is most appropriate to treat malabsorption?','["A. Lactase enzyme supplement","B. Enteral feeding via nasogastric tube","C. Bile acid sequestrant (colestyramine)","D. Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (PERT) with meals"]','D','Chronic pancreatitis leads to exocrine pancreatic insufficiency. Treatment of fat malabsorption: pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (PERT — e.g., Creon/pancrelipase) taken with meals and snacks to aid digestion of fat, protein, and carbohydrate. Fat-soluble vitamin supplementation (A, D, E, K) is also needed. Alcohol cessation is essential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Jaundice & Liver Disease','easy','mcq','A 25-year-old man presents with jaundice after a febrile illness: predominantly unconjugated bilirubin raised, normal ALT, normal ALP, Hb 8.5 g/dL, and reticulocytosis. What is the most likely type of jaundice?','["A. Pre-hepatic (haemolytic) jaundice","B. Obstructive (post-hepatic) jaundice","C. Hepatocellular jaundice","D. Cholestatic jaundice"]','A','Pre-hepatic (haemolytic) jaundice is characterised by: predominantly unconjugated (indirect) hyperbilirubinaemia; normal liver enzymes; evidence of haemolysis (anaemia, reticulocytosis, low haptoglobin); urine — no bilirubin (unconjugated bilirubin is not water-soluble and cannot be filtered by the kidney), but increased urobilinogen.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Jaundice & Liver Disease','medium','mcq','A 50-year-old woman has jaundice, pale stools, dark urine, and intense pruritus. ALT is mildly elevated but ALP and GGT are markedly elevated. Ultrasound shows dilated intrahepatic bile ducts. What pattern of liver disease does this represent?','["A. Hepatocellular pattern (hepatitis)","B. Cholestatic (obstructive) pattern","C. Haemolytic pattern","D. Infiltrative pattern"]','B','This is a cholestatic (obstructive/post-hepatic) pattern: markedly elevated ALP and GGT with mildly elevated transaminases, conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia (dark urine), pale stools (absent bile in gut), and pruritus (retained bile salts in skin). Dilated bile ducts on ultrasound confirm biliary obstruction. Causes include gallstones, cholangiocarcinoma, pancreatic head cancer, and primary sclerosing cholangitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Jaundice & Liver Disease','easy','mcq','A 19-year-old man has recurrent mild jaundice during fasting or illness. All liver enzymes and FBC are normal. Only unconjugated bilirubin is mildly elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Rotor syndrome","B. Dubin-Johnson syndrome","C. Gilbert syndrome","D. Crigler-Najjar syndrome"]','C','Gilbert syndrome is the commonest hereditary cause of mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia (benign condition). It is caused by reduced UGT1A1 enzyme activity. Bilirubin rises during fasting, illness, or stress. All other liver tests are normal, and there is no haemolysis. No treatment is required — it is a benign condition requiring only reassurance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Jaundice & Liver Disease','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old man presents with acute jaundice, ALT 1200 U/L, AST 1100 U/L, and mildly elevated ALP 150 U/L. He takes paracetamol regularly for back pain. Which investigation is most important?','["A. Abdominal ultrasound","B. Liver biopsy","C. MRCP","D. Serum paracetamol level and viral hepatitis serology"]','D','Massively elevated transaminases (ALT/AST in thousands) with mildly elevated ALP suggests hepatocellular pattern — causes include viral hepatitis, paracetamol/drug hepatotoxicity, ischaemic hepatitis, and autoimmune hepatitis. Serum paracetamol level and drug history are critical; paracetamol hepatotoxicity can occur with therapeutic doses in certain settings (malnutrition, chronic alcohol use, enzyme-inducing drugs).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Jaundice & Liver Disease','hard','mcq','A 20-year-old woman presents with jaundice, tremor, psychiatric symptoms, and haemolytic anaemia. Examination reveals golden-brown rings at the corneal margins. Serum caeruloplasmin is low. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Wilson''s disease","B. Haemochromatosis","C. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","D. Autoimmune hepatitis"]','A','Wilson''''s disease (autosomal recessive ATP7B mutation) presents in young patients with: hepatic disease, neuropsychiatric symptoms (tremor, dysarthria, psychiatric disturbance), Kayser-Fleischer rings (golden-brown deposits at Descemet''''s membrane — pathognomonic), and Coombs-negative haemolytic anaemia. Low serum caeruloplasmin (<0.2 g/L) supports the diagnosis. Treatment: D-penicillamine or trientine (copper chelation), zinc.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Jaundice & Liver Disease','medium','mcq','In obstructive jaundice, which investigation sequence is most appropriate?','["A. ERCP immediately","B. Abdominal ultrasound followed by MRCP if non-diagnostic","C. CT abdomen with contrast alone","D. Liver biopsy"]','B','The investigation of choice in obstructive jaundice starts with abdominal ultrasound (USS): it can identify dilated bile ducts, gallstones, and pancreatic masses, and is non-invasive. If USS is non-diagnostic or further delineation is needed, MRCP provides excellent biliary anatomy without radiation and without the risk of therapeutic ERCP. ERCP is reserved for when therapeutic intervention is needed (stone extraction, stenting).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Jaundice & Liver Disease','easy','mcq','Which liver function test pattern is most consistent with hepatocellular damage (e.g., viral hepatitis)?','["A. ALP 600 U/L, GGT 500 U/L, ALT 55 U/L","B. Unconjugated bilirubin 80 µmol/L, ALT 35 U/L, ALP 70 U/L","C. ALT 850 U/L, AST 900 U/L, ALP 120 U/L","D. ALP 450 U/L, GGT 480 U/L, conjugated bilirubin 120 µmol/L"]','C','Hepatocellular damage (viral hepatitis, drug toxicity, autoimmune hepatitis) causes predominantly elevated transaminases (ALT and AST) out of proportion to ALP — the hepatocellular pattern. In cholestatic disease, ALP and GGT are markedly elevated relative to transaminases. ALT/ALP ratio >1.5 suggests hepatocellular; <0.5 suggests cholestatic.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Jaundice & Liver Disease','hard','mcq','A 45-year-old man presents with progressive jaundice and weight loss over 3 months. CT abdomen shows a 3 cm mass in the head of the pancreas with a dilated common bile duct. CA 19-9 is markedly elevated. Which investigation provides tissue diagnosis and local staging in one procedure?','["A. Abdominal ultrasound","B. MRCP","C. CT-guided percutaneous biopsy","D. EUS (endoscopic ultrasound) with fine needle aspiration"]','D','Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) with fine-needle aspiration (FNA) is the preferred investigation for tissue diagnosis of a pancreatic head mass: it provides high-resolution imaging, local vessel involvement (staging), and allows FNA biopsy in the same procedure. EUS-FNA has higher sensitivity than CT-guided biopsy for pancreatic lesions and avoids the risk of peritoneal seeding.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Jaundice & Liver Disease','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and obesity has elevated ALT 95 U/L, AST 80 U/L, and mildly elevated ALP 130 U/L. Hepatitis B and C serologies are negative. Ultrasound shows a bright, echogenic liver. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD)","B. Primary biliary cholangitis","C. Autoimmune hepatitis","D. Primary sclerosing cholangitis"]','A','Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is the most common liver disease in developed countries and is strongly associated with obesity, type 2 diabetes, and metabolic syndrome. Ultrasound showing a bright (hyperechogenic) liver is consistent with hepatic steatosis. Management: lifestyle modification (weight loss 5-10% reduces hepatic steatosis), treatment of metabolic comorbidities.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Jaundice & Liver Disease','hard','mcq','A 38-year-old man with haemochromatosis has serum ferritin 2500 µg/L, transferrin saturation 85%, and liver biopsy confirming cirrhosis with marked iron deposition. Which is the most appropriate long-term treatment?','["A. Oral iron chelation with deferasirox","B. Regular venesection (phlebotomy) weekly until ferritin <50 µg/L, then maintenance venesection","C. Liver transplantation immediately","D. Desferrioxamine infusion twice weekly"]','B','Hereditary haemochromatosis is treated by therapeutic venesection (phlebotomy): 450-500 mL blood removed weekly until ferritin <50 µg/L and transferrin saturation <30%. Maintenance venesection (every 3-4 months) is continued lifelong. Early treatment prevents progression to cirrhosis. In established cirrhosis, venesection reduces the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma. Oral iron chelators are used when phlebotomy is not possible.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Liver Cirrhosis - Complications','easy','mcq','A 55-year-old man with alcohol-related liver cirrhosis presents with increasing abdominal distension. Ascitic tap: protein 8 g/L, neutrophil count 100 cells/mm³. What is the first-line pharmacological management of his ascites?','["A. Furosemide 40 mg daily alone","B. IV albumin infusion","C. Spironolactone 100 mg daily (first-line), with furosemide added if needed","D. Terlipressin and fluid restriction"]','C','Spironolactone is the first-line diuretic for cirrhotic ascites: it antagonises aldosterone, which is elevated in cirrhosis due to secondary hyperaldosteronism. Starting dose: 100 mg/day, titrated up to 400 mg/day. Furosemide (40 mg) can be added if inadequate response, maintaining a 100:40 spironolactone:furosemide ratio to prevent hypokalaemia. Sodium restriction (88 mmol/day, ~2 g/day) is essential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Liver Cirrhosis - Complications','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old woman with liver cirrhosis undergoes diagnostic paracentesis. Ascitic fluid shows: neutrophil count 320 cells/mm³, protein 12 g/L, culture pending. What is the most likely diagnosis and appropriate treatment?','["A. Malignant ascites; chemotherapy","B. Chylous ascites; low-fat diet","C. Tuberculous peritonitis; anti-TB therapy","D. Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP); IV cefotaxime or ciprofloxacin"]','D','Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP) is diagnosed when ascitic fluid neutrophil count ≥250 cells/mm³. It is the most common spontaneous infection in cirrhotic patients. Common organisms: E. coli, Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneumoniae. Treatment: IV cefotaxime 2 g TDS or ciprofloxacin for 5 days. IV albumin (1.5 g/kg on day 1, 1 g/kg on day 3) reduces the risk of hepatorenal syndrome and mortality.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Liver Cirrhosis - Complications','medium','mcq','A cirrhotic patient with known oesophageal varices has never had a variceal bleed. Which treatment is most appropriate for primary prophylaxis?','["A. Either non-selective beta-blocker or endoscopic band ligation","B. Oral propranolol or carvedilol (non-selective beta-blocker)","C. Endoscopic band ligation only if varices are large","D. IV terlipressin"]','A','Primary prophylaxis of variceal haemorrhage in patients with medium/large varices: either non-selective beta-blocker (propranolol or carvedilol — reduce cardiac output and splanchnic vasoconstriction, reducing portal pressure) or endoscopic variceal band ligation (EVL). Both are equally effective. Terlipressin is used for acute variceal haemorrhage treatment, not primary prophylaxis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Liver Cirrhosis - Complications','hard','mcq','A 50-year-old man with decompensated cirrhosis develops confusion, asterixis, and altered consciousness after a recent GI bleed. Ammonia level is elevated. What is the most appropriate initial management?','["A. IV thiamine and glucose, then lactulose and rifaximin","B. Identify and treat precipitant; give lactulose to produce 2-3 soft stools/day; add rifaximin","C. IV lorazepam for agitation and sedation","D. Urgent liver transplant listing"]','B','Hepatic encephalopathy (HE) management: (1) Identify and treat precipitants — GI bleed, infection (SBP), constipation, electrolyte imbalance, sedative drugs; (2) Lactulose (10-30 mL TDS) targeting 2-3 soft stools/day — reduces gut ammonia production; (3) Rifaximin 550 mg BD added for maintenance and prevention of recurrence; (4) Nutritional support (do not restrict dietary protein). Sedatives worsen HE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Liver Cirrhosis - Complications','easy','mcq','Which scoring system is used to prioritise patients with liver cirrhosis for liver transplantation?','["A. Child-Pugh score","B. Glasgow Coma Scale","C. MELD (Model for End-stage Liver Disease) score","D. Rockall score"]','C','The MELD (Model for End-stage Liver Disease) score is used internationally to prioritise patients on the liver transplant waiting list — higher scores indicate more severe liver disease and higher short-term mortality risk. MELD uses: serum creatinine, bilirubin, and INR. The Child-Pugh score (using bilirubin, albumin, PT, ascites, and encephalopathy) is used for prognostic classification (Class A/B/C) but not for transplant allocation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Liver Cirrhosis - Complications','medium','mcq','A 58-year-old man with cirrhosis undergoes a 5-litre paracentesis. What should be co-administered to prevent paracentesis-induced circulatory dysfunction?','["A. IV normal saline 500 mL","B. IV furosemide 80 mg","C. IV terlipressin 1 mg","D. IV albumin 8 g per litre of ascites removed"]','D','Paracentesis-induced circulatory dysfunction (PICD) occurs after large-volume paracentesis (>5 litres). Prevention: IV albumin 8 g per litre of ascites removed should be given simultaneously with large-volume paracentesis. Albumin expands the intravascular volume and prevents the haemodynamic consequences of rapid fluid removal.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Liver Cirrhosis - Complications','hard','mcq','A 62-year-old woman with cirrhosis develops progressive renal failure over 2 weeks. Urine protein is negative, no nephrotoxin exposure, no hypovolaemia. Urine sodium is <10 mmol/L and creatinine rises to 320 µmol/L. What is the most likely diagnosis and recommended treatment?','["A. Hepatorenal syndrome type 1; terlipressin plus IV albumin","B. Acute tubular necrosis; IV crystalloid resuscitation","C. Pre-renal acute kidney injury; IV crystalloids alone","D. Contrast nephropathy; N-acetylcysteine"]','A','Hepatorenal syndrome (HRS) is functional renal failure in cirrhotic patients with no intrinsic renal pathology. Type 1 HRS: rapidly progressive (creatinine doubles to >226 µmol/L in <2 weeks). Treatment: terlipressin (vasoconstrictor reducing splanchnic vasodilation) plus IV albumin 20-40 g/day. Definitive treatment: liver transplantation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Liver Cirrhosis - Complications','medium','mcq','During an episode of SBP in a cirrhotic patient, which intervention reduces the risk of hepatorenal syndrome and mortality?','["A. IV furosemide 80 mg during SBP episodes","B. IV albumin (1.5 g/kg day 1, 1 g/kg day 3) given simultaneously with antibiotics during SBP","C. Immediate TIPS insertion","D. Oral terlipressin"]','B','IV albumin infusion given alongside antibiotics during an episode of SBP significantly reduces the risk of hepatorenal syndrome (HRS) and improves survival. Evidence from the Sort et al. (1999) trial shows that albumin 1.5 g/kg on day 1 and 1 g/kg on day 3 prevents the circulatory dysfunction that can precipitate HRS in SBP. This is a grade A recommendation in EASL cirrhosis guidelines.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Liver Cirrhosis - Complications','easy','mcq','What is the most common precipitant of hepatic encephalopathy in a patient with cirrhosis?','["A. High dietary protein intake","B. Use of lactulose","C. Gastrointestinal bleeding","D. Physical exercise"]','C','Gastrointestinal bleeding (including variceal haemorrhage or peptic ulcer) is the most common precipitant of hepatic encephalopathy in cirrhotic patients. Blood in the gut is digested and produces large amounts of ammonia, which the failing liver cannot clear. Other precipitants: infection (especially SBP), constipation, electrolyte disturbances, sedatives/opioids, and renal failure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Liver Cirrhosis - Complications','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old cirrhotic man with oesophageal varices has had two variceal bleeds despite beta-blocker therapy and endoscopic band ligation. His MELD score is 15. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. Switch to sclerotherapy","B. Start rifaximin and lactulose","C. Surgical portosystemic shunt","D. TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt) insertion"]','D','TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt) is indicated for secondary prophylaxis of variceal haemorrhage in patients who rebleed despite optimal combination therapy (beta-blockers + EVL). TIPS creates a low-resistance channel between the portal and hepatic veins, dramatically reducing portal pressure. It is effective in >90% of cases but carries risks: hepatic encephalopathy (20-30%), shunt stenosis, and hepatic decompensation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Liver Failure','medium','mcq','Which of the following correctly defines acute liver failure?','["A. Jaundice, coagulopathy (INR >1.5), and hepatic encephalopathy developing within 26 weeks in a patient with NO pre-existing liver disease","B. Cirrhosis with hepatic encephalopathy in a patient with known chronic liver disease","C. Elevated transaminases >10 times the upper limit of normal without encephalopathy","D. Viral hepatitis causing jaundice lasting more than 6 months"]','A','Acute liver failure (ALF) is defined as the development of jaundice + coagulopathy (INR >1.5) + hepatic encephalopathy within 26 weeks in a patient with no pre-existing liver disease. Key classifications by time from jaundice to encephalopathy: hyperacute (<7 days — paracetamol, hepatitis A/E), acute (7-28 days), and subacute (4-26 weeks — drug reactions, indeterminate).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Liver Failure','easy','mcq','What is the most common cause of acute liver failure in the United Kingdom?','["A. Hepatitis B virus infection","B. Paracetamol (acetaminophen) overdose","C. Hepatitis E virus","D. Autoimmune hepatitis"]','B','Paracetamol (acetaminophen) overdose is the most common cause of acute liver failure in the UK, accounting for approximately 60-70% of ALF cases. Other causes in the UK include: idiosyncratic drug reactions, viral hepatitis (A, B, E), autoimmune hepatitis, and Wilson''''s disease. In sub-Saharan Africa and South/Southeast Asia, viral hepatitis B and E are the predominant causes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Liver Failure','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old woman presents 36 hours after intentionally taking 30 paracetamol 500 mg tablets. She is jaundiced with INR 4.5, creatinine 380 µmol/L, and grade 3 encephalopathy. What is the specific antidote?','["A. Activated charcoal","B. Methionine","C. N-acetylcysteine (NAC) intravenous infusion","D. Flumazenil"]','C','N-acetylcysteine (NAC) is the specific antidote for paracetamol hepatotoxicity. It works by replenishing glutathione, which is depleted by the toxic metabolite NAPQI. NAC is most effective within 8 hours of ingestion but should be given even in late presentations with established ALF, as it improves transplant-free survival.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Liver Failure','hard','mcq','A 28-year-old man presents with ALF from paracetamol overdose. Arterial pH is 7.28 after resuscitation. Creatinine is 310 µmol/L, PT is 108 seconds. He has grade 3 hepatic encephalopathy. According to King''''s College Criteria for paracetamol-induced ALF, should he be listed for liver transplantation?','["A. No; pH criterion alone is not sufficient","B. No; all three criteria must be met simultaneously","C. Yes; but only after 72 hours of observation","D. Yes; arterial pH <7.3 alone after adequate resuscitation meets the King''s College Criteria"]','D','King''''s College Criteria (KCC) for paracetamol-induced ALF: Transplant if EITHER: (1) Arterial pH <7.3 after adequate resuscitation (regardless of grade of encephalopathy); OR (2) All three of: PT >100 seconds (INR >6.5) + creatinine >300 µmol/L + grade 3-4 encephalopathy. This patient has pH 7.28 which satisfies criterion 1 alone, and should be listed.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Liver Failure','medium','mcq','What is the characteristic feature of hepatitis E in pregnancy?','["A. It causes severe, potentially fatal ALF in pregnant women, particularly in the third trimester","B. It is universally benign and self-limiting in all populations","C. It causes chronic infection in immunocompetent patients","D. It is the most common cause of ALF in the UK"]','A','Hepatitis E (HEV) causes a self-limiting illness in most immunocompetent patients, but is particularly dangerous in pregnancy — especially in the third trimester — where it can cause fulminant hepatic failure with mortality rates of 20-25%. Hepatitis E is the most common cause of acute viral hepatitis in sub-Saharan Africa and is transmitted faeco-orally (contaminated water).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Liver Failure','easy','mcq','In acute liver failure, which coagulation test best reflects hepatic synthetic function and is used in prognostic scoring?','["A. Platelet count","B. Prothrombin time (PT) or INR","C. Activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT)","D. Fibrinogen level"]','B','Prothrombin time (PT) or its international normalised ratio (INR) is the best indicator of hepatic synthetic function in acute liver failure, as factors II, V, VII, IX, and X are all synthesised exclusively in the liver. A progressively rising PT/INR indicates worsening hepatic synthetic failure and is a key component of both the King''''s College Criteria and the MELD score.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Liver Failure','hard','mcq','King''''s College Criteria for liver transplant listing in NON-paracetamol acute liver failure requires PT >100 seconds; OR any THREE of five adverse criteria. Which of the following is NOT one of the five adverse criteria?','["A. Age <10 years or >40 years","B. Serum bilirubin >300 µmol/L","C. Serum sodium <130 mmol/L","D. PT >50 seconds"]','C','The King''''s College Criteria for non-paracetamol ALF (any 3 of 5): (1) Age <10 or >40 years; (2) Aetiology: drug reaction, Wilson''''s disease, or indeterminate (not hepatitis A/B); (3) Duration of jaundice to encephalopathy >7 days; (4) Serum bilirubin >300 µmol/L; (5) PT >50 seconds. Serum sodium <130 mmol/L is NOT one of the five KCC criteria for non-paracetamol ALF.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Liver Failure','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old man with ALF from hepatitis B has grade 2 encephalopathy and INR of 3.5. When should fresh frozen plasma (FFP) be given?','["A. Give FFP immediately to correct INR to <1.5","B. Give platelet transfusion instead","C. Give vitamin K IV to correct the coagulopathy","D. Do not give FFP routinely; only give if there is active bleeding or an invasive procedure is planned"]','D','In ALF, coagulopathy (elevated PT/INR) should NOT be routinely corrected with FFP because: (1) PT/INR is used as a dynamic prognostic marker — normalising it removes this important clinical tool; (2) FFP carries risks (volume overload, allergic reactions, TRALI). FFP is given ONLY if there is active bleeding or a planned invasive procedure. Vitamin K IV should be given as patients may be vitamin K deficient.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Liver Failure','hard','mcq','What is the most common cause of death in patients with acute liver failure?','["A. Cerebral oedema and raised intracranial pressure","B. Variceal haemorrhage","C. Hepatorenal syndrome","D. Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis"]','A','Cerebral oedema with raised intracranial pressure (ICP) is the most common cause of death in acute liver failure, particularly in hyperacute ALF (e.g., paracetamol). It results from accumulation of ammonia and glutamine in astrocytes, causing cellular swelling. Clinical features: hypertension, bradycardia (Cushing reflex), decerebrate posturing. Management: mannitol, hypertonic saline, head elevation, ICP monitoring.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Acute Liver Failure','easy','mcq','A patient with ALF is in the ICU. Which of the following is a critical aspect of supportive management?','["A. Early introduction of high-protein enteral nutrition","B. Monitoring for and preventing hypoglycaemia with IV dextrose infusion","C. Prophylactic broad-spectrum antibiotics given routinely to all ALF patients","D. Therapeutic anticoagulation with heparin to prevent DIC"]','B','Hypoglycaemia is a life-threatening complication of ALF (the liver cannot perform gluconeogenesis) and must be actively monitored (hourly glucose checks) and treated with IV 10-50% dextrose infusions. Other critical supportive measures: treat cerebral oedema, prevent/treat infection, maintain MAP >75 mmHg with vasopressors, renal replacement therapy for AKI, correct electrolytes, avoid sedatives.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Pancreatitis','easy','mcq','A 45-year-old obese woman presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, vomiting, and fever. Serum lipase is 5 times the upper limit of normal. What are the two most common causes of acute pancreatitis worldwide?','["A. Autoimmune disease and hypercalcaemia","B. Medications and trauma","C. Gallstones and alcohol","D. Hypertriglyceridaemia and ERCP"]','C','Gallstones and alcohol account for approximately 75-80% of all cases of acute pancreatitis worldwide. Gallstones (biliary pancreatitis) are the most common cause overall. Other causes: hypertriglyceridaemia (>1000 mg/dL), hypercalcaemia, medications (azathioprine, thiazides, valproate), ERCP, and trauma. The mnemonic I GET SMASHED is used to remember all causes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Pancreatitis','medium','mcq','A 50-year-old man with acute pancreatitis has: WBC 17 × 10⁹/L, blood glucose 13 mmol/L, urea 18 mmol/L, calcium 1.9 mmol/L, LDH 700 U/L, albumin 29 g/L, and PaO2 7.5 kPa. Using the Glasgow/Imrie criteria (PANCREAS mnemonic), what does his score indicate?','["A. 3 criteria; mild pancreatitis","B. Score is inconclusive without 48-hour values","C. 2 criteria; mild pancreatitis manageable on the ward","D. 5 or more criteria; severe pancreatitis requiring HDU admission"]','D','Glasgow/Imrie criteria (PANCREAS mnemonic, assessed within 48 hours): PaO2 <8 kPa (yes, 7.5), Age >55 (no, age 50), Neutrophils WBC >15 (yes, 17), Calcium <2 mmol/L (yes, 1.9), Urea >16 mmol/L (yes, 18), Enzyme LDH >600 (yes, 700), Albumin <32 g/L (yes, 29), Sugar >10 mmol/L (yes, 13). Score ≥3 indicates SEVERE pancreatitis, requiring HDU/ICU admission.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Pancreatitis','medium','mcq','Which enzyme is more sensitive and specific than serum amylase for diagnosing acute pancreatitis?','["A. Serum lipase","B. Serum trypsinogen","C. Faecal elastase-1","D. Serum phospholipase A2"]','A','Serum lipase is more sensitive (94-96%) and specific (96-99%) than serum amylase for the diagnosis of acute pancreatitis. Lipase remains elevated for longer (7-10 days vs 3-5 days for amylase), making it more useful for delayed presentations. Serum amylase is also elevated in other conditions (salivary gland disease, perforated viscus, bowel obstruction, renal failure), reducing its specificity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Pancreatitis','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old woman with acute gallstone pancreatitis develops worsening pain, fever (38.8°C), and rising CRP at day 10. CT scan shows 40% pancreatic necrosis with gas bubbles in the necrotic tissue. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. Sterile necrosis; continue conservative management","B. Infected necrotising pancreatitis; CT-guided percutaneous drainage ± surgical or endoscopic necrosectomy","C. Pancreatic pseudocyst; endoscopic drainage","D. Pancreatic abscess; IV antibiotics alone"]','B','Gas in pancreatic necrotic tissue on CT is pathognomonic of infected pancreatic necrosis. Management: (1) Broad-spectrum antibiotics with good pancreatic penetration (meropenem, imipenem); (2) Drainage — the step-up approach is preferred: CT-guided percutaneous drainage first, followed by minimally invasive necrosectomy (video-assisted retroperitoneal debridement — VARD — or endoscopic transgastric necrosectomy) if drainage fails.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Pancreatitis','easy','mcq','What is the Balthazar CT severity index used for in acute pancreatitis?','["A. To determine whether ERCP is indicated","B. To assess exocrine pancreatic function","C. To grade the severity of pancreatitis and predict complications based on CT findings","D. To guide surgical timing"]','C','The Balthazar CT Severity Index (CTSI) grades acute pancreatitis based on CT findings: Balthazar grade (A-E: A=normal pancreas to E=extensive extrapancreatic inflammation + fluid collections) combined with degree of pancreatic necrosis. CTSI score ≥7 correlates with high complication rate and mortality. CT is performed when diagnosis is uncertain or in severe pancreatitis failing to improve at 48-72 hours.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Pancreatitis','medium','mcq','A 48-year-old man with gallstone pancreatitis has his amylase return to normal. When should cholecystectomy be performed to prevent recurrence?','["A. Immediately during the acute episode","B. Only if there is another episode of pancreatitis","C. After 6-8 weeks when inflammation has completely resolved","D. During the same hospital admission or within 2 weeks of discharge"]','D','For mild gallstone pancreatitis, laparoscopic cholecystectomy should be performed during the same hospital admission (or within 2 weeks of discharge) to prevent recurrence. Delaying cholecystectomy is associated with a high risk of recurrent biliary events (30% within 6 weeks). For severe pancreatitis with complications, cholecystectomy is delayed until after clinical recovery (typically 6-8 weeks).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Pancreatitis','hard','mcq','A 60-year-old woman with gallstone pancreatitis develops fever, jaundice, and rigors on day 3 of admission. ALT is 380 U/L and ALP 520 U/L. What does this combination suggest and what is the immediate management?','["A. Ascending cholangitis secondary to common bile duct stone; urgent ERCP within 24 hours","B. Infected pancreatic necrosis; surgical debridement","C. Hepatitis B reactivation; start antiviral therapy","D. Drug-induced hepatitis; stop all medications"]','A','Fever, rigors, and jaundice (Charcot''''s triad) with a biliary obstruction pattern (elevated ALP, ALT) in the context of gallstone pancreatitis indicates acute ascending cholangitis — a life-threatening infection requiring urgent intervention. Management: (1) IV antibiotics (e.g., piperacillin-tazobactam); (2) Urgent ERCP within 24 hours (within 12 hours for severe cholangitis/shock) to drain the common bile duct.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Pancreatitis','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis from alcohol excess has recurrent pain, steatorrhoea, and weight loss. What is the most appropriate pancreatic enzyme replacement to manage his malabsorption?','["A. Enteric-coated pancrelipase taken 30 minutes before meals","B. Enteric-coated pancrelipase (e.g., Creon) taken with meals and snacks","C. Non-enteric-coated pancreatin with antacids","D. Lipase enzyme supplement once daily at bedtime"]','B','In chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency, enteric-coated pancrelipase (e.g., Creon 25,000-50,000 units per meal) should be taken WITH meals and snacks to mix with food in the duodenum. Enteric coating protects enzymes from gastric acid. If response is inadequate, a proton pump inhibitor can be added. Fat-soluble vitamin supplementation is essential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Pancreatitis','easy','mcq','Within what time frame does serum amylase typically rise after the onset of acute pancreatitis, and when does it return to normal?','["A. Rises immediately (within 30 minutes); normalises by 24 hours","B. Rises within 24-48 hours; normalises by 7-10 days","C. Rises within 2-12 hours; normalises by 3-5 days","D. Rises within 6-24 hours; may remain elevated for 2-3 weeks"]','C','Serum amylase rises rapidly within 2-12 hours of the onset of acute pancreatitis and returns to normal within 3-5 days. A normal amylase does NOT exclude acute pancreatitis if the patient presents late (>3-5 days after onset). Lipase is preferred in late presentations as it remains elevated for 7-10 days. A serum amylase >3 times the upper limit of normal is considered significant.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Pancreatitis','hard','mcq','A 65-year-old woman develops a 5 cm asymptomatic pancreatic pseudocyst 6 weeks after acute pancreatitis. What is the most appropriate management?','["A. Immediate surgical cystojejunostomy","B. CT-guided percutaneous drainage","C. Urgent endoscopic transpapillary drainage via ERCP","D. Conservative management (watchful waiting) as most resolve spontaneously"]','D','Asymptomatic pancreatic pseudocysts can be managed conservatively (watchful waiting) as the majority (up to 70%) resolve spontaneously, particularly when <6 cm and not increasing in size. Intervention is indicated if the pseudocyst is symptomatic (pain, early satiety, biliary obstruction), enlarging, infected, or complicated (haemorrhage, rupture). When drainage is needed, endoscopic ultrasound-guided cystogastrostomy is the preferred approach.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Colorectal Cancer & Screening','easy','mcq','A 58-year-old man presents with a 3-month history of rectal bleeding, a change in bowel habit to looser stools, and a 5 kg weight loss. What is the most appropriate initial investigation?','["A. Colonoscopy with biopsy","B. CT colonography","C. Faecal immunochemical test (FIT)","D. Flexible sigmoidoscopy"]','A','Colonoscopy with biopsy is the gold standard investigation for suspected colorectal cancer: it allows direct visualisation of the entire colon, biopsy of suspicious lesions, and polypectomy in the same procedure. This patient has red flag symptoms (rectal bleeding + change in bowel habit >6 weeks, age >40, weight loss) mandating urgent two-week wait referral for colonoscopy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Colorectal Cancer & Screening','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old woman has a 3 cm ulcerated sigmoid colon adenocarcinoma on colonoscopy. CT shows no lymph node involvement and no distant metastases, but the tumour extends through the muscularis propria into pericolorectal tissues. What is the TNM stage?','["A. T2 N0 M0","B. T3 N0 M0","C. T4 N0 M0","D. T3 N1 M0"]','B','TNM staging of colorectal cancer: T3 = tumour extends through the muscularis propria into pericolorectal tissues (not into adjacent organs); N0 = no regional lymph node metastasis; M0 = no distant metastasis. T1 = invades submucosa; T2 = invades muscularis propria; T3 = into pericolorectal tissue; T4a = penetrates visceral peritoneum; T4b = invades adjacent structures. This is equivalent to Dukes B / Stage II.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Colorectal Cancer & Screening','easy','mcq','Which faecal marker is used as the primary colorectal cancer screening test in national population screening programmes in high-income countries?','["A. Faecal occult blood test (guaiac-based)","B. Faecal calprotectin","C. Faecal immunochemical test (FIT)","D. Carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) in stool"]','C','The faecal immunochemical test (FIT) is used as the primary screening test in most national colorectal cancer screening programmes in high-income countries. FIT specifically detects human haemoglobin in stool using antibodies and is more sensitive and specific than guaiac-based FOBT. A positive FIT result triggers colonoscopy for further investigation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Colorectal Cancer & Screening','hard','mcq','A 42-year-old man has had three colorectal cancers, diagnosed before age 50. His family history includes his father and two uncles with colorectal cancer before age 50. Genetic testing confirms a pathogenic variant in MLH1. What is the diagnosis and recommended surveillance?','["A. Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP); annual colonoscopy from age 13","B. Serrated polyposis syndrome; annual colonoscopy","C. MUTYH-associated polyposis; biennial colonoscopy from age 18","D. Lynch syndrome (HNPCC); colonoscopy every 1-2 years from age 25"]','D','Lynch syndrome (Hereditary Non-Polyposis Colorectal Cancer — HNPCC) is caused by germline mutations in mismatch repair genes: MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2. Features: early-onset CRC (<50 years), multiple CRC in same individual, extracolonic cancers (endometrial, ovarian, gastric, urinary tract). Surveillance: colonoscopy every 1-2 years starting at age 25 (or 5 years before youngest affected relative).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Colorectal Cancer & Screening','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old man has stage III (Dukes C) colon cancer after right hemicolectomy with 3/14 lymph nodes positive. What adjuvant treatment is recommended?','["A. Adjuvant chemotherapy with FOLFOX or CAPOX for 6 months","B. Radiotherapy to the right hemicolon","C. Surveillance colonoscopy at 3 months with no systemic treatment","D. Targeted therapy with bevacizumab alone"]','A','Stage III (Dukes C — lymph node positive) colon cancer is an indication for adjuvant chemotherapy after curative resection. Standard regimens: FOLFOX (5-fluorouracil + leucovorin + oxaliplatin) or CAPOX/XELOX (capecitabine + oxaliplatin) for 6 months. Adjuvant chemotherapy reduces the risk of relapse by approximately 25-30%. Radiotherapy is used for rectal cancer (pre-operative), not typically for colon cancer.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Colorectal Cancer & Screening','medium','mcq','Which of the following is the most significant red flag feature prompting urgent referral for suspected colorectal cancer in a 45-year-old patient?','["A. Haemorrhoidal bleeding in a 25-year-old with no change in bowel habit","B. Rectal bleeding with a change in bowel habit to looser stools lasting >6 weeks","C. Occasional bright red blood on the toilet paper after straining","D. Single episode of abdominal cramping with one loose stool"]','B','Rectal bleeding combined with a change in bowel habit (looser stools and/or increased frequency) persisting for >6 weeks is the most significant red flag combination for colorectal cancer, particularly in patients aged >40 years. It requires urgent two-week wait (2WW) referral for colonoscopy. Other red flags: unexplained iron-deficiency anaemia, palpable rectal or abdominal mass, and unexplained weight loss with change in bowel habit.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Colorectal Cancer & Screening','hard','mcq','A 65-year-old woman with resected stage IV colorectal cancer (RAS/BRAF wild-type, KRAS wild-type) has liver-only metastases. What targeted therapy can be considered in addition to chemotherapy?','["A. Imatinib (Gleevec)","B. Trastuzumab (anti-HER2)","C. Cetuximab or panitumumab (anti-EGFR antibodies)","D. Pembrolizumab for all stage IV CRC"]','C','Cetuximab and panitumumab are anti-EGFR targeted therapies effective in metastatic colorectal cancer — but ONLY in patients with RAS (KRAS and NRAS) and BRAF wild-type tumours. Mutations in KRAS/NRAS (present in ~50% of CRC) predict resistance to anti-EGFR therapy. Pembrolizumab (PD-1 inhibitor) is approved for MSI-high (mismatch repair deficient) mCRC as first-line therapy. Bevacizumab (anti-VEGF) can be used irrespective of RAS status.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Colorectal Cancer & Screening','easy','mcq','What is the recommended interval and age range for population-based colorectal cancer screening with the FIT in high-income countries?','["A. Annual FIT from age 40-80 years","B. FIT every 3 years from age 45-70 years","C. FIT every 5 years from age 60-75 years","D. FIT every 2 years from age 50-74 years"]','D','Most national population-based colorectal cancer screening programmes offer FIT every 2 years to individuals aged 50-74 years. A positive FIT result (above the cut-off threshold) triggers colonoscopy. Population screening reduces CRC mortality by approximately 15-25% through early detection of cancers and adenomas.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Colorectal Cancer & Screening','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old woman has CT colonography showing a 7 mm polyp in the sigmoid colon. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. Colonoscopy with polypectomy","B. Repeat CT colonography in 3 years","C. Faecal immunochemical test to assess if the polyp is bleeding","D. Proceed directly to surgical resection"]','A','CT colonography identifies colonic polyps but cannot remove them or obtain biopsies. For polyps found on CTC: >10 mm — colonoscopy for polypectomy/biopsy; 6-9 mm — colonoscopy or surveillance CTC at 3 years (depending on patient risk); <5 mm — surveillance CTC at 5 years or discharge. This 7 mm polyp falls in the 6-9 mm category and colonoscopy with polypectomy is indicated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Clinicals','Colorectal Cancer & Screening','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old man has rectal cancer 8 cm from the anal verge, staged T3 N1 M0 on MRI. What is the preferred treatment approach?','["A. Surgery alone (anterior resection) without neoadjuvant therapy","B. Long-course chemoradiotherapy followed by surgery 8-12 weeks later","C. Chemotherapy followed by surgery at 6 months","D. Palliative radiotherapy and best supportive care"]','B','Locally advanced rectal cancer (T3/T4 or N+) is managed with neoadjuvant (preoperative) long-course chemoradiotherapy (45-50.4 Gy with concurrent capecitabine or 5-FU) followed by surgery 8-12 weeks later to allow tumour downstaging. This approach reduces local recurrence rates and may allow sphincter-preserving surgery. Short-course radiotherapy (25 Gy in 5 fractions) is an alternative for selected patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th Ed');
